[32m+[m[32mWorkflow Overview[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 1: Model Training[m
[32m+[m[32mThe machine learning model is trained on a dataset (train.csv) containing features related to the risk of insurance claims. The model used is XGBoost, a scalable and powerful model for prediction tasks.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mCode Structure:[m
[32m+[m[32mapp.py: The main script to deploy the model as an API.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mtrain.csv: Dataset for model training.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mbest_xgb_model.pkl: Trained XGBoost model (saved using pickle).[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mfull_pipeline.pkl: Preprocessing pipeline used to transform input data before prediction.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 2: API Deployment(app.py)[m
[32m+[m[32mThe model is exposed as a Flask API. The /predict endpoint accepts JSON input data, preprocesses it, and returns a prediction.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 3: Version Control & Collaboration[m
[32m+[m[32mThe project uses Git for version control and GitHub as the hosting platform. Teams can work on different branches for feature development, and merge them once the feature is complete.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mGit LFS is used to handle large files, such as the trained model (best_xgb_model.pkl), which exceeds GitHub’s file size limit of 100MB.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mHow to Use Git LFS (Important for large files):[m
[32m+[m[32mTrack large files with Git LFS: The model files are tracked using Git LFS to prevent issues with GitHub’s file size limits.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mClone the repository with LFS support: Ensure LFS is installed and tracked before cloning the repo.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mTo install Git LFS, follow instructions here: Git LFS Setup.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mAfter installation, clone the repository and pull all LFS files:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mbash[m
[32m+[m[32mCopy[m
[32m+[m[32mEdit[m
[32m+[m[32mgit lfs install[m
[32m+[m[32mgit clone https://github.com/Datkhoo25/insurance_risk_prediction.git[m
[32m+[m[32mUsing LFS in your workflow: When pushing or pulling files, LFS automatically manages large files. For example:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mbash[m
[32m+[m[32mCopy[m
[32m+[m[32mEdit[m
[32m+[m[32mgit push origin main[m
[32m+[m[32mgit pull origin main[m
[32m+[m[32mUntracked large files: If you add a large file that is not being tracked by LFS, you can stage it for LFS tracking using:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mbash[m
[32m+[m[32mCopy[m
[32m+[m[32mEdit[m
[32m+[m[32mgit lfs track "*.pkl"[m
[32m+[m[32mgit add .gitattributes  # To commit the changes in .gitattributes[m
[32m+[m[32mgit add <file>[m
[32m+[m[32mgit commit -m "Add model to LFS"[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 4: Pushing to GitHub[m
[32m+[m[32mTo share updates, push your changes to GitHub.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mbash[m
[32m+[m[32mCopy[m
[32m+[m[32mEdit[m
[32m+[m[32mgit add .[m
[32m+[m[32mgit commit -m "Add model and preprocessing pipeline"[m
[32m+[m[32mgit push origin main[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 5: Using Docker for Consistency[m
[32m+[m[32mThe application can be containerized using Docker, which ensures that the model works consistently in any environment (e.g., AWS, local machines).[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mCreate Dockerfile: Define how to build and run the application.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mBuild Docker image: To containerize the application, build the image:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mbash[m
[32m+[m[32mCopy[m
[32m+[m[32mEdit[m
[32m+[m[32mdocker build -t insurance-risk-prediction .[m
[32m+[m[32mRun Docker container: After building the Docker image, run it to start the Flask API:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mbash[m
[32m+[m[32mCopy[m
[32m+[m[32mEdit[m
[32m+[m[32mdocker run -p 5000:5000 insurance-risk-prediction[m
